                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ctxt
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma
                  ,\;
                  X
                  ,\;
                  Y
                  \\;\;\;
                  ctxt
                  \\,,
          %p
            which classically may always be thought of as a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pair+type"}> dependent
            ) classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+type"} product type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ctxt
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma
                  \\times
                  X
                  \\times
                  Y
                  \\;\;\;
                  ctxt
                  \\,,
          %p
            so a context in bunched logic is allowed, more generally, to be a nested list of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rule"} structural
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+connective"} connectives
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ctxt
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A,\;
                  B,\;
                  \\big(
                  C;\;
                  (D,\,E);
                  \\;
                  F
                  \\big),\;
                  (G;\,H)
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  ctxt
                  \\,.
          %p
            The original and common motivation for such “bunched contexts” is the existence of further (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
            and)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
            %em in addition
            to the classical ones; such as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theories"} linear type theories
            with their additional (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+implication"} linear implication
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\multimap (-)
            and)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+conjunction"} linear conjunction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\otimes (-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubly+closed+monoidal+category"} doubly closed monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+products"} cartesian products
            and another
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ×
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ctxt
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A
                  \\times
                  B
                  \\times
                  \\big(
                  C
                  \\otimes
                  (D \times E)
                  \\otimes
                  F
                  \\big)
                  \\times
                  (G \otimes H)
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  ctxt
                  \\,.
          %blockquote
            %p (NB: In saying this we are reversing the role of commas and semicolons as compared to the original literature on bunched logic, so that the comma retains it original meaning as the classical connective.)
          %p
            Accordingly, the bunched context indicates a variation of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            will admit the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction rule
            and/or the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            may not.
          %p
            Notice that the general phrase
            %em bunched logic
            is not entirely standard, and the word “bunches” has been used with more than one logic of this form.
          %p
            The notion (together with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubly+closed+monoidal+categories"} doubly closed monoidal categories
            = succeed "-calculus\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \lambda
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda-calculus"} lambda-calculus
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#O'HearnPym99"} O’Hearn &amp; Pym (1999)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Pym2002"} Pym (2002)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#O'Hearn03"} O’Hearn (2003)
          %p#Unsatisfactory
            But aspects of the proposal of
            %a{:href => "#O'HearnPym99"} O’Hearn &amp; Pym (1999)
            and
            %a{:href => "#Pym2002"} Pym (2002)
            have remained unsatisfactory, see
            %a{:href => "#Pym08Errata"} Pym (2008), Errata to Pym (2002)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Riley22Thesis"} Riley(2022)
            %a{:href => "https://mvr.hosting.nyu.edu/pubs/thesis.pdf#page=29"} pp. 19
            and Rem. 1.4.1 (
            %a{:href => "https://mvr.hosting.nyu.edu/pubs/thesis.pdf#page=64"}> p. 64
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://mvr.hosting.nyu.edu/pubs/thesis.pdf#page=29"}> Riley 2022, pp. 19
              \&amp;rbrack;: &amp;lbrack;Since&amp;rbrack; context manipulations are not recorded in the terms, typechecking a term can be difficult. First, it is not always obvious when and how the contraction rule has to be applied, and secondly, it is not always obvious how the context has to be split into two pieces to apply
              = succeed "-ELIM." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
            %p
              The various extensions of the αλ-calculus that have since appeared &amp;lbrack;BO06; CPR08; SS04; Atk04&amp;rbrack; do not resolve these issues. It is also not so clear how to add
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
              to this system and how dependency should behave between bunches.
          %p Further developments had not resolved the fundamental issue:
          %blockquote
            %p{:id => "UnsatisfactorySchöppStark"}
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://mvr.hosting.nyu.edu/pubs/thesis.pdf#page=101"}> Riley 2022, p. 91
              \:&amp;rbrack; Schöpp and Stark’s Bunched Dependent Type Theory &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#SchöppStark04"}> Schöpp &amp; Stark (2004)
              \&amp;rbrack; &amp;lbrack;admits&amp;rbrack; no dependency across bunches. The corresponding monoidal pair type therefore requires the input types to be closed. &amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; the monoidal unit is the terminal object. This is certainly not the case in &amp;lbrack;linear&amp;rbrack; models of interest.
          %p
            In reaction to this,
            %a{:href => "#Riley22Thesis"} Riley (2022)
            proposes an enhanced form of bunched type theory which is claimed to fix all these problems at least in the case that the second structural connective
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            %a{:href => "#Riley22Thesis"} Riley (2022)
            to make this work are:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+modality"} classical modality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♮
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \natural
                which from any “parameterized linear type” projects out the underlying classical type;
            %li
              %p a decoration of bunched contexts by “color palettes” which keep explicit track of the bunching/tree structure.
          %p
            (On the other hand, this second point leads to a proliferation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#bunched_implications Bunched implications
          %p
            In
            %strong bunched implications
            logic (
            %a{:href => "#O'HearnPym99"}> O’Hearn &amp; Pym (1999)
            ), the semicolon allows contraction and weakening while the comma does not. This allows defining both an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+conjunction"} additive conjunction
            and a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+law"} distribute
            over the “”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+disjunction"} additive disjunction
            and moreover both come with a corresponding implication: the “additive”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+implication"} multiplicative implication
          %h3#relevance_logic Relevance logic
          %p
            Some forms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relevance+logic"} relevance logic
            can be presented with a bunched sequent calculus that is similar to BI, but in which the comma also has contraction (though not weakening) and there is no additive implication. See for instance (
            %a{:href => "#Mints"}> Mints
            ).
          %h3#classical_bunched_implication Classical bunched implication
          %p
            A logic of
            %strong classical bunched implication
            is like BI but with arbitrary bunches on the right as well as on the left. On the right, the semicolon represents the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+disjunction"} additive disjunction
            and the comma represents a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+disjunction"} multiplicative disjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            that move formulas back and forth. Both negations are “classical” with respect to their corresponding connectives, e.g. we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim\sim A \multimap A
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            is the multiplicative negation and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
            the multiplicative implication) and also
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg A \to A
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
            is the additive negation and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            the additive implication). See
            %a{:href => "#Pym2002"} Pym 2002
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/4004/paraconsistent-logic/?Focus=57557#Comment_57557"} this discussion
          %h3#dependent_versions Dependent versions
          %p
            Bunched logics are also used to combine linear type theories with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
          %h2#categorical_semantics Categorical semantics
          %p
            Bunched logics naturally have semantics in categories with more than one
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+structure"} monoidal structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ;
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ;
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,(B;C),((D,E);F))
            can be interpreted as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes (B\boxtimes C) \otimes ((D\otimes E)\boxtimes F)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            one. A typical and motivating example of a model for BI is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            over a monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ccc+structure+on+presheaves"} ccc structure on presheaves
            as well as the closed monoidal structure given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubly+closed+monoidal+category"} doubly closed monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relevance+logic"} relevance logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+logic"} separation logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/display+logic"} display logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substructural+logic"} substructural logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+topos"} monoidal topos
          %h2#references References
          %p Precursors:
          %ul
            %li#Mints
              G. E. Mints.
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cut-elimination theorem for relevant logics
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/archive.phtml?wshow=paper&jrnid=znsl&paperid=2569&option_lang=eng"} math-net.ru
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://doi.org/10.1007/BF01084083"} doi:10.1007/BF01084083
          %p The original articles:
          %ul
            %li{:id => "O'HearnPym99"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+O%27Hearn"}> Peter O'Hearn
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+J.+Pym"} David J. Pym
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Logic of Bunched Implications
                %strong 5
                2 (1999) 215-244 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.lsv.ens-cachan.fr/~demri/OHearnPym99.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/421090"} doi:10.2307/421090
            %li#Pym2002
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Pym"}> David Pym
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Semantics and Proof Theory of the Logic of Bunched Implications
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 26
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-017-0091-7"} doi:10.1007/978-94-017-0091-7
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Semantics_and_Proof_Theory_of_the_Lo.html?id=0bAfqhzDuOcC&redir_esc=y"} GoogleBooks
              %p#Pym08Errata
                Errata and Remarks for
                %em The Semantics and Proof Theory of the Logic of Bunched Implications
                (2008) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.cantab.net/users/david.pym/BI-monograph-errata.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Pym-BI-Errata.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  \&amp;lbrack;
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Pym-BI-Errata.pdf#page=5"}> p. 5
                  \&amp;rbrack; “Items 3, 4, and 5 in the errata for Chapter 12 and 4, 5 in Chapter 13, whilst necessary to fix a technical problem, leave us in a conceptually and computationally undesirable situation: we have no clean characterization of a useful notion of well-formedness, leading to obvious computational difficulties in, say, theorem proving. We conclude that such a highly multiplicative system is far too complex, at least in its current formulation, to be of much value. All of the applications of multiplicative quantifiers known to-date require much simpler systems. It is the author’s recommendation that readers should not devote very much of their resources to studying the system of predicate BI discussed in these chapters”
            %li{:id => "O'Hearn03"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+O%27Hearn"}> Peter O'Hearn
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On bunched typing
                %strong 13
                4 (2003) 747-796 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796802004495"}> doi:10.1017/S0956796802004495
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Review and further development:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Bodil Biering,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the logic of bunched implications – and its relation to separation logic
